// `Mapper<Int>` and `Mapper<String>` are DIFFERENT types. Roadmap A9, and this is the fixture for
// the vtable-keying decision.
//
// Monomorphising an interface makes each instantiation an ordinary interface under a mangled name,
// so the question "how is the vtable keyed" answers itself: by the interface name, which is what
// it was always keyed by, and `Mapper$Int` and `Mapper$String` are two names. Nothing in the
// vtable machinery learned that generics exist.
//
// What that buys is exactly this refusal. A class that implements one instantiation does not
// implement the other, and passing it where the other is wanted is a type error rather than a
// call through the wrong slot — which is the failure mode a shared vtable would have produced,
// silently and at runtime.
//
// The message names the interface as the AUTHOR wrote it — `Mapper<String>`, never the mangled
// `Mapper$String` the compiler made up.
interface Mapper<T> { function apply(self, x: T) -> T }

class Doubler { }
implement Mapper<Int> for Doubler {
    function (self) apply(x: Int) -> Int { return x * 2; }
}

let d: Doubler = Doubler { };
let wrong: dynamic Mapper<String> = d;
print(wrong.apply("hi"));
